# No credential leaks (GitHub)

Secrets must never enter Git history or reach GitHub. Protect against leaks before every commit and every push.

## Never push credentials to GitHub

- Do **not** `git add`, commit, or `git push` `.env`, `.env.*` (except committed `.env.example` placeholders), `creds.log`, `credential.json`, `*.pem`, private keys, wallet mnemonics, or any file with live tokens/API keys.
- Do **not** put real secrets in tracked source, docs, test fixtures, or `open-dome-lib/test/`.
- Local secrets stay in gitignored files only (`.env`, `creds.log`, local app `scripts/` except CI helpers).
- Examples and `.env.example` use placeholders only (`YOUR_API_KEY`, `process.env.SECRET`).
- If a leak is found in the working tree or in `LAST..HEAD`: **stop**, do not commit/push, tell the user which file and secret **type** (never echo the secret).

## `creds.log` review (session start + before commit)

`creds.log` (gitignored) stores the last commit SHA already reviewed for leaked credentials.

1. Read `creds.log`. The first `^[0-9a-f]{7,40}$` line is `LAST`.
2. Run `git rev-parse HEAD` and `git log --oneline LAST..HEAD`.
3. If that range is non-empty, scan `git diff LAST..HEAD` for secrets before other work.
4. After a clean review, overwrite `creds.log` with the new HEAD SHA (keep the comment header).
5. Never `git add` or commit `creds.log`.
6. Never commit app `scripts/` except `update-packages.js` / `update-version.js` (CI). Test/smoke/sweep scripts stay local.
